A||||9||WisconsinGT||Surging while holding accelerator steady||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||09-18-2000||12:36 AM||||Has anyone had similar symptoms? If so, do you have any info. on this problem. By the way, I have replaced fuel regulator. Had injectors blueprinted. And installed TorqueMaster plugs. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||wkayl||09-18-2000||10:34 AM||||If you are running tha A/C when it does this, you may be low on freon.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-18-2000||12:00 PM||||The A6 compressor can do that even when it's not low on refrigerant. Some people just notice it more than others. Turn off AC and see if it stops. Could be  refrigerant or the compressor itself getting wornout. (A6's take allot of energy to kick over when the clutch grabs even when they are working right.)<P>could also be TC lockup acting up. You can unplug the connector of the front of the tranny and see if it stops.<P>If it seems to do it under certain loads, like going up an incline, check the motor mounts, dog bone, and cradle bushings. Sometimes what feels like surging is actually the motor/tranny set oscilating in the mounts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||09-18-2000||12:11 PM||||Mine does that too, and my A/C compressor dioesn't work. But I know the dogbone is shot, got an ultimate dogbone on order. <P>But it does it even sitting still.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||09-18-2000||04:37 PM||||Thanks guys! I do not have the AC on when this happens. Also, it happens on flat roadways as well. FWI...I have a new Fiero Store Dog bone on my car. Three weeks now. I will check the TC Lockup. Any other ideas? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||09-18-2000||05:38 PM||||I have the exact same problem, but only between 1900 and 2000RPM, with steady speed. I would be interested to hear what this could be.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||09-18-2000||05:49 PM||||Mine does the same thing, but only when the engine is operating in closed loop, and speeds are anywhere under 30mph. I just assumed it was my leaky injectors.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||09-18-2000||06:22 PM||||just out of curiosity, have you checked your fuel filter? or if you can check the fuel preassure. The best thing to do is elliminate everything one part at a time. it sounds like a fuel problem. pump, filter, injectors. or it could be a slight miss, don't the coils make similar symptoms when their going? could be spark plug wires. If you can rule out the fuel and electrical systems then all that's left is air (intake/exhaust) and the computer related stuff. partially plugged cat? EGR? ahh well, just some things that popped in my head.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-18-2000||08:13 PM||||Is it something you feel or is it showing on the tach?<P>If you feel it but RPM is constant then I'd look at the motor & cradle mounts very carefully. Especially those of you with polly torque struts in. My 87SC 4 cyl drove me about nuts with this until I put polly in the strut. It still did it under certain light loads after that. Putting polly in the cradle has nearly eliminated it. Now the only time it works the mounts is when I lug it right on the edge of a down shift but that's to be expected.<P><BR>If RPM is bouncing then you have a whole diferant problem. There are a few things that could cause this. <P>Start with the simple one and disable the TC lock. If it turns out to be TC lock then you might look into the lock delay modul from <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/" TARGET=_blank>Transmission Exchange Co.</A>. ((You may not notice an RPM change if it's the TC. The diferance is about 200rpm from locked to unlocked)) The delay modul keeps the TC from locking for a second or 3 after the ECM enables it. (direct link to delay modul <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/shftrite.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.txchange.com/shftrite.htm</A> Ask them if you have more questions about it. They used to have more information than this but I couldn't find it. )<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||09-18-2000||09:16 PM||||I have an '88 v-6 5-speed. '88s don't have cradle bushings, I have poly dog-bone, and all new engine/tranny mounts. The surging doesn't show up in the tach, but is very noticeable, ie: keys in the ignition rock back and forth, and if I have a passenger you can just see their legs rocking back and forth along with the keys in the ignition. Most annoying.||||reg||1
